# Part Five — The Revision Sheet

> The whole topic on a few pages. Read it the night before a practice paper and in the last week before the exam.

## The discipline (Head I)

::: table | Head I in one table.
@widths 3.4,10.6
Line | What to remember
What Gender Studies is | Multidisciplinary study of how gender is **constructed**, how it shapes **power**, and how it **intersects** with class, religion, race, sexuality. Aims: make visible, explain, question, inform policy, empower. Needed because knowledge was androcentric (Gilligan), women's work uncounted (Waring), the movement's questions unanswered, "add women and stir" not enough.
Women's vs Gender Studies | WS: late 1960s (Cornell 1969, SDSU 1970, NWSA 1977); women as subject, knower, beneficiary. GS: 1980s–90s; gender relations; includes men, masculinities, sexualities. Drivers: gender concept (Oakley, Rubin, Scott), men's studies (Connell), queer theory, critique of "woman" (hooks, Mohanty), donors' WID→GAD shift. Eight criteria: subject, concept, origin, aim, politics, standpoint, theory, institutions. Critics: "feminism without women" (Modleski 1991). Verdict: stages and partners of one project.
Multidisciplinary nature | Multi (side by side) vs inter (blended) vs trans (beyond). Sociology (Oakley), anthropology (Mead, Ortner, Rosaldo), psychology (Bem, Gilligan), history (Lerner), economics (Boserup, Waring), politics (Pateman), law (MacKinnon), biology (intersex, health), literature/linguistics (Showalter, Lakoff), media (Mulvey), development (Moser, Kabeer), religion (Wadud, Mernissi). Strength: complete picture. Challenge: "no core", institutional home → autonomy debate.
Autonomy vs integration | Bowles and Klein (1983). Autonomy: space, depth, protection, legitimacy, critical voice; risk ghettoisation. Integration: relevance everywhere, reach, transformation, cheaper, engages men; risk dilution. Resolution: dual strategy (the model for Beijing's twin-track gender mainstreaming). Contributions: identity, feminist epistemology (Harding 1986), institutions, curriculum transformation, mainstreaming model, self-criticism. Pakistan: both.
Status in Pakistan | Before: Shirkat Gah (1975), WAF (1981), Aurat Foundation (1986), Mumtaz and Shaheed (1987). 1989: centres at QAU, Karachi, Punjab, Peshawar, Balochistan. Achievements: degrees, research, journals, professionals, policy (NPDEW 2002, GRAP), CSS 2016. Problems: funding, faculty, Western curriculum, weak research, resistance, ghettoisation, weak policy links. Way forward: HEC funding, faculty, local research and language, integration, policy links, men, intersectional research.
:::

## The social construction of gender (Head II)

::: table | Head II in one table.
@widths 3.4,10.6
Line | What to remember
Historicizing constructionism | Essentialism (Aristotle; Victorian science) → Wollstonecraft 1792 → Mill 1869 ("eminently artificial") → Mead 1935 → Beauvoir 1949 → Money 1955 → Berger and Luckmann 1966 → Garfinkel 1967 → Stoller 1968 → Oakley 1972 → Rubin 1975 → Foucault 1976 → Kessler and McKenna 1978 → West and Zimmerman 1987 → Butler 1990 → Lorber 1994.
Theories of construction | Psychoanalytic (Freud, Chodorow) · social learning (Bandura, Mischel) · cognitive-developmental (Kohlberg: identity, stability, constancy) · gender schema (Bem 1981) · interactionist / doing gender (Goffman, West and Zimmerman) · performativity (Butler) · structural (Lorber, Connell, Risman). Agents: family, school, religion, media, state. Critique: ignores body; passive children; relativism; universals; abstraction (Nussbaum).
Deconstructing "gender" | Derrida's binaries. Eight meanings: grammatical; synonym for sex; roles; identity; performance; institution/structure; relation of power (Scott 1986); policy category. Masculine/feminine binary is hierarchical, not natural, unstable.
Gendered language | Reflects, reproduces, can change hierarchy. Forms: generic masculine; marked female terms; semantic derogation (Schulz); insults and idioms ("choorian pehen lo"; "zan, zar, zameen"); naming; victim-blaming; conversation (Lakoff, Zimmerman and West, Tannen). Spender: *Man Made Language*. Implications: invisibility, stereotyping, devaluation, normalised violence, silencing.
Queer theory and "sex" | Origins c. 1990 (Queer Nation; de Lauretis; Butler; Sedgwick; Foucault). Claims: categories historical; heteronormativity; fluidity; deconstruct binaries; study margins. Is sex constructed? Yes: Laqueur's one-sex model; intersex (0.018%–1.7% depending on definition); medical decisions; sports testing; Butler's "always already gender". No: reproduction is binary; most fit; classification ≠ creation; real needs. Middle: bodies real, meanings social. *Khuntha* in fiqh.
Masculinities and femininities | Plural, relational, ranked. Connell: hegemonic, complicit (patriarchal dividend), marginalised, subordinated; emphasised femininity. Roles come from: biology, economy (plough — Boserup; Alesina et al.), property (Engels), religion and culture, law and state, socialisation and media. Costs: violence, men's health, breadwinner burden, emotional suppression.
Nature vs culture | Nature: chromosomes, hormones, CAH studies, average differences, sociobiology (Wilson), evolutionary psychology (Buss), neuroandrogenic theory (Ellis). Culture: Mead, historical change, Baby X (Condry 1976), socialisation, brain plasticity (Fine 2010; Joel 2015). David Reimer case. Ortner (1974). Consensus: interactionism.
Transgender debate | 1871 Criminal Tribes Act; SC 2009 (Khaki); Census 2017: 10,418; Act 2018 (s. 3 self-perceived identity; s. 7 inheritance); controversy 2022; FSC 19 May 2023; appeal pending 2026. Balance: dignity and protection (Arts 14, 25); disputed question to courts and parliament.
:::

## The five answer frames that repeat

::: fig grid | The five frames that answer most Topic 1 questions.
@cols 5
Define and differentiate: definitions; history; 8-criteria table; examples; relationship
Status in Pakistan: history; institutions; achievements; problems; way forward
Autonomy vs integration: origin; case for each; risks; dual strategy; Pakistan
Theories of construction: seven theories; agents; critique; interactionist view
Natural or social?: biology; culture; Reimer; Ortner; interactionism; Pakistan
:::

## Ten names to drop in any Topic 1 answer

Beauvoir (1949) · Oakley (1972) · Bowles and Klein (1983) · Joan Scott (1986) · Connell (1987/1995) · West and Zimmerman (1987) · Butler (1990) · Lorber (1994) · Mead (1935) · Ortner (1974).
